<header><p class="enc-kicker">Place · island</p><h1 class="enc-title">Walcheren</h1></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc">Walcheren is a former island in the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/dutch">Dutch</a> province of Zeeland, at the mouth of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/scheldt">Scheldt</a>, now joined to the mainland by reclaimed land. Its port of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/vlissingen">Vlissingen</a> (Flushing) commanded the approach to <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/antwerp">Antwerp</a>. In 1809 a British expedition of about 40,000 men landed there to strike at the French fleet, but it was crippled by 'Walcheren fever' and withdrew. Allied forces flooded and captured the island in 1944.</p><figure class="enc-fig"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/walcheren.jpg" alt="Walcheren" width="320" height="240" fetchpriority="high"><figcaption>Image: Zeelandia at Dutch Wikipedia (Original text: Zeelandia eigen werk) · CC BY-SA 3.0 · <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Kampveerse_toren.jpg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Wikimedia Commons</a></figcaption></figure></div>
<section class="enc-more"><h2 class="enc-more-h">Setting</h2><p>Walcheren is a region and former island in Zeeland at the mouth of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/scheldt">Scheldt</a> estuary, between the Eastern Scheldt in the north and the Western Scheldt in the south, roughly rhombus-shaped. Its two sides facing the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/north-sea">North Sea</a> are dunes and the rest of its coast is dykes. <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/middelburg">Middelburg</a>, the provincial capital, lies at its centre, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/vlissingen">Vlissingen</a> is the main harbour 9 kilometres to the south, and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/veere">Veere</a> is the third municipality in the north.</p><p>The island was joined to its neighbours by later works: the Sloedam, built in 1871 for a railway, and polders made after the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/second-world-war">Second World War</a> connected it to Zuid-Beveland, and the Veerse Gatdam, completed in 1961, linked it to Noord-Beveland. The name is first attested in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/latin">Latin</a> spellings such as villam Walichrum about 790, and probably combines a word for moist with one for a sandy ridge.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">History</h2><p>In Roman times the island was a point of departure for ships going to <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/britain">Britain</a> and had a temple of the goddess Nehalennia, popular with those crossing the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/north-sea">North Sea</a>. In the ninth century it was the seat of the Danish Viking Harald, who conquered part of what became the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/kingdom-of-the-netherlands">Netherlands</a> with his brother Rorik. In 1588 <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/dutch">Dutch</a> rebels and English fighters held Walcheren, which blocked access to <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/antwerp">Antwerp</a> and denied the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/spanish-armada">Spanish Armada</a> a deep-water port for supplies and shelter. Under the Secret Treaty of Dover of 1670 <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/england">England</a> was to receive Walcheren, but the plan failed when Dutch resistance repulsed the French and English attack.</p><p>On 30 July 1809 a British expeditionary force landed to attack the French fleet at Flushing and to assist the Austrians against <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/napoleon">Napoleon</a>. British troops captured Flushing, but the Austrians had been defeated at Wagram in early July and the French fleet had moved to <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/antwerp">Antwerp</a>. The force lost over 4,000 men to Walcheren Fever, thought to be a combination of malaria and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/typhus">typhus</a>, against 106 killed by enemy action, and withdrew in December.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">The place today</h2><p>Walcheren was fought over in 1940 and again in 1944, in the final stage of the Battle of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/scheldt">Scheldt</a>, because it commanded the route to <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/antwerp">Antwerp</a>. On 3 October 1944 the RAF bombed the sea wall at Westkapelle, flooding the island. Canadian troops fought along the narrow Walcheren Causeway from 31 October, and British Commandos landed at Westkapelle on 1 November in Operation Infatuate to silence the German coastal batteries. All German resistance on the island had ceased by 8 November 1944.</p><p class="enc-more-src">This account follows the Wikipedia article “Walcheren” (CC BY-SA 4.0), linked under Read further below.</p></section>
<section class="enc-quotes"><h2 class="enc-section-title">Across the Institute</h2><ul><li><blockquote>Merchants of Vlissingen in Zeeland, led by Jan de Moor, sent settlers to Tobago in 1628 under a patent from the Dutch West India Company and called the island New <b>Walcheren</b>.</blockquote><a href="/state/dutch-tobago">Dutch Tobago</a></li><li><blockquote>The economy suffered nonetheless, and the British landed on <b>Walcheren</b> in 1809.</blockquote><a href="/state/kingdom-of-holland">Kingdom of Holland</a></li></ul></section>
